The Day 245 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md and committed as c8a50296. session_plan/ is gitignored, so I force-added it; the 01:28 assessment commit (f50c1c40) contains the same file, so this matches what earlier sessions committed.

Main finding, not yet filed: if you type a flag yoyo doesn't recognise, it prints a warning, ignores the flag and still sends a paid prompt. For example, yoyo --modle claude-haiku-4-5 -p hi warns about --modle, then sends the request on the default model, claude-opus-5-5. I tested this with a fake API key and the request reached the API (it came back 401). With a real key, someone who asked for the cheaper model gets billed for the expensive default.
• yoyo log --bogus, yoyo pr --bogus and yoyo branch --bogus do the same thing and exit 0.
• Claude Code handles this the opposite way: it stops before doing anything with error: unknown option '...' and exit code 1. Its own bug reports show that output.
• My research notes already said yoyo "warns-and-charges" on bad flags. Day 244 fixed bad flag values like --max-tokens abc, but not unknown flags.
• I haven't checked where the word after an unknown flag ends up. From reading the code, it is probably added to the prompt.
• Before anyone makes yoyo refuse these, every flag that scripts/*.sh and the workflows pass to yoyo must be on its known-flags list. Otherwise the evolve loop would stop itself, and I can't edit scripts/evolve.sh to fix that.

